---

/**
 * Hero override.
 *
 * Says what the site *is* — free, open ground school for helicopter pilots and
 * the people teaching them. It deliberately does not
 * pitch the pedagogy: "learn it in building blocks" was selling a method to
 * someone who has not yet decided to be here, and the method shows itself once
 * they are reading a course.
 *
 * It says **ground school**, not "flight training". That is not a stylistic
 * preference: 14 CFR 61.1 defines flight training as instruction received from
 * an authorized instructor *in flight in an aircraft*, and ground training as
 * everything else. No website can deliver the former — it is a category error,
 * not just a loose word — and on a site whose credibility rests on being
 * precise about regulations, the headline was the worst place to be imprecise.
 * "Ground school" stays correct even once `kind: flight` lessons exist, because
 * what a page can carry is the briefing for a flight lesson, never the lesson.
 *
 * Both actions point at index pages rather than a specific course, so neither
 * goes stale as courses are added.
 *
 * The buttons name the reader's *intent*, not our two content collections.
 * "Browse courses" / "View subject areas" named the taxonomy, and to someone
 * who has never been here that reads as whole-and-part — "a course is the
 * thing, subjects must be bits of it" — which is not the distinction at all.
 * The real axis is teaching order versus reference, so: "Start a course" and
 * "Look something up".
 *
 * The subline sets up that fork in the same order as the buttons: first clause
 * to the first button, second clause to the second. Both use the wording the
 * section indexes already use about themselves, so the button and the page it
 * lands on agree.
 *
 * Do not "fix" these labels back to match the section headings. The headings are
 * taxonomy and should stay Courses / Subject Areas; the buttons are an
 * invitation and should stay intent.
 *
 * Four elements, and no fifth. There used to be a rounded "pill" above the
 * headline reading "Community built · Open source · Always free", with a green
 * status dot. It went because it said nothing the next two lines do not say
 * better: "free" and "open" are in the headline, "community-written" and "no
 * accounts, no ads, no paywalls" are in the subline. A reader met the same three
 * claims three times before reaching a button. The dot was worse than
 * redundant — a green dot is the convention for "service is up", which this was
 * not reporting.
 *
 * If something new is proposed for this hero, the test is whether it says
 * something the headline and subline do not.
 *
 * The last line does two jobs neither of the others does: it names who writes
 * this, and it says the work is ongoing. Authorship is there because the site
 * used to say "community-written", which stopped being true when the source
 * went private — and a named, credentialled instructor is a far stronger signal
 * to a student than "community" ever was, because "community" could mean
 * anyone. Make it plural when that is actually the case, and not before.
 *
 * ATP and Gold Seal CFII are aviation credentials, not jargon: this audience
 * reads them the way a developer reads a well-known library. The ban on jargon
 * is about *our plumbing* — repositories, hosting, build steps — not about the
 * subject the site teaches. Note the phrasing keeps "helicopter" attached to
 * "instructor" and lists the certificates separately, so it does not claim a
 * rotorcraft ATP specifically.
 *
 * It deliberately does **not** count anything.
 *
 * It briefly did — a computed "2 lessons and 1 reference article so far". That
 * was accurate and self-maintaining, and still wrong for a launch: a specific
 * small number is an anchor a reader cannot un-see, where "lesson by lesson"
 * sets the same expectation without inviting arithmetic. The honest scale is
 * still one click away on the course page, which counts lessons and weeks from
 * the collection. Put a count back here when the numbers flatter the work;
 * `CourseCatalog.astro` has the counting rule (a four-part lesson is one
 * lesson).
 */
---

<section class="ck-hero">
	<h1>Free, open ground school<br class="ck-br" /> for helicopter pilots.</h1>

	<p class="ck-sub">
		Week-by-week lessons in the order a student actually needs them — plus
		reference articles that cover a subject in full, for looking something up.
		For student pilots, and the instructors teaching them.
	</p>

	<div class="ck-actions">
		<a href="/curriculum/" class="ck-btn ck-btn-primary">Start a course</a>
		<a href="/subjects/" class="ck-btn ck-btn-secondary">Look something up</a>
	</div>

	<p class="ck-scale">
		Written lesson by lesson by a working helicopter instructor — ATP, Gold Seal CFII.
	</p>
</section>

{/*
  Outside the hero, and the only links on this page besides the two actions.

  Every other page reaches these from the sidebar footer, but the home page uses
  Starlight's `splash` template and therefore renders no sidebar at all — so
  without this, the one page a first-time reader lands on is the only page with
  no route to the licence or the disclaimer. That is also the page where someone
  wondering "what's the catch?" goes looking.

  This is not the fine print that was removed. That was a hedge in the hero
  ("study material only — not an FAA-approved course"), which undersold the
  work. These are two quiet links.
*/}
<p class="ck-home-links">
	<a href="/start-here/why-this-is-free/">Why this is free</a>
	<span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
	<a href="/start-here/disclaimer/">Disclaimer &amp; licensing</a>
</p>

<style>
	@reference "../../styles/global.css";

	.ck-hero {
		max-width: 46rem;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: clamp(3rem, 10vmin, 6rem) 0 clamp(1rem, 3vmin, 2rem);
		text-align: center;
	}

	.ck-hero h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5.4vw, 3.25rem);
		font-weight: 700;
		line-height: 1.08;
		letter-spacing: -0.035em;
		color: var(--color-text-primary);
	}

	.ck-sub {
		margin: 1.15rem auto 0;
		max-width: 34rem;
		font-size: 1.0625rem;
		line-height: 1.65;
		color: var(--color-text-secondary);
	}

	.ck-actions {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		margin-top: 1.85rem;
	}

	/* Quiet on purpose. It is a fact a reader should have, not a selling point
	   and not an apology. */
	.ck-scale {
		margin: 1.35rem 0 0;
		font-size: 0.8125rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.ck-home-links {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		margin: 3.5rem 0 0;
		font-size: 0.8125rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.ck-home-links a {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.ck-home-links a:hover {
		color: var(--color-accent);
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
	}

	.ck-br {
		display: none;
	}

	/* ─── RESPONSIVE ───────────────────────────────────────
	   The headline sizes itself with clamp(), so only the things clamp cannot
	   express get a breakpoint. */

	/* The hand-placed line break only helps once the line is long enough to
	   want breaking; below this it fights the natural wrap. */
	@media (width >= theme(--breakpoint-sm)) {
		.ck-br {
			display: inline;
		}
	}

	/* Phones: two side-by-side buttons wrap to two lines of different widths,
	   which reads as a mistake. Stack them full-width instead — and they become
	   proper thumb targets at the same time. */
	@media (width < theme(--breakpoint-xs)) {
		.ck-actions {
			flex-direction: column;
			align-items: stretch;
			gap: 0.5rem;
		}
	}
</style>
